.TH BATON 1 "October 2026" "baton 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
baton \- compile and run programs written in the baton language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B baton
.I command
.RI [ file .bt ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B baton
compiles the baton language to C and builds the result with gcc. Baton is a
statically typed, general-purpose, low-level language; the language itself is
defined in
.BR baton-spec.md ,
which ships with this package and can be read with
.BR "baton man" .
.PP
Every command that takes a
.I file.bt
argument compiles that file to C, writes the C next to it, and calls gcc.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI build " file.bt"
Compile to C, then gcc to a binary.
.TP
.BI run " file.bt"
Build and run.
.TP
.BI check " file.bt"
Stop after type checking; no C is written and gcc is not called.
.TP
.BI man " [section]"
Show the language specification. The section can be a number or part of a
title, so
.B man 13
shows "Memory management" and
.B man memory
finds it too. The specification is written in Markdown, but it is rendered as
plain text for the terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Output binary path (default: the source name without .bt).
.TP
.BI \-W " none|error"
Silence warnings, or promote them to errors.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-c
With build: write the C and stop before gcc.
.TP
.B \-\-list
With man: list the spec sections and stop.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
With man: print the specification as Markdown instead of as text.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
With man: write the spec straight to stdout.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/baton.1
This manual page.
.TP
.I /var/lib/yapm/packages/baton
The compiler itself: the python package, the specification and the examples.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BATON_SPEC
Path of the specification file to read. The package sets it, so that
.B baton man
finds the specification.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
baton run examples/hello.bt
baton build hello.bt -o hello -W error
baton man 7
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gcc (1)
